<subsection xml:id="subsec-linear-first-order-odes">
  <title>Linear First-Order ODEs</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      A first-order ODE is <em>linear</em> when <m>y</m> and <m>y'</m> appear
      only to the first power and are never multiplied together or fed into
      nonlinear functions.
      Linear ODEs arise throughout engineering: RC circuits, mixing problems,
      Newton's law of cooling, and many more.
      Their key feature is that an explicit general solution formula always
      exists, derived by a technique called <em>variation of constants</em>.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ─── Definition ─── -->

  <definition xml:id="def-linear-first-order-ode">
    <title>Linear First-Order ODE; Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        A first-order ODE is <term>linear</term> if it can be written as
        <me>
          y'(t) + a(t)\, y(t) = b(t),
        </me>
        where <m>a(t)</m> and <m>b(t)</m> are given functions of <m>t</m>.
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              If <m>b(t) = 0</m>, the ODE is <term>homogeneous</term>.
              Every homogeneous linear first-order ODE is also separable.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              If <m>b(t) \neq 0</m>, the ODE is <term>inhomogeneous</term>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <!-- ─── Classification example ─── -->

  <example xml:id="ex-linear-ode-classification">
    <title>Classifying Linear ODEs</title>
    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>y'(t) + e^{4t} y(t) = y(t)</m>,
            equivalently <m>y' + (e^{4t} - 1)y = 0</m>.
            <em>Linear, homogeneous</em>:
            <m>a(t) = e^{4t} - 1</m>, <m>b(t) = 0</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>y'(t) + \sin(4t \cdot y(t)) = 0</m>.
            <em>Not linear</em>: <m>y</m> appears as the argument of
            <m>\sin</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>y'(t) + t\, y(t) = t^4</m>.
            <em>Linear, inhomogeneous</em>:
            <m>a(t) = t</m>, <m>b(t) = t^4</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </example>

  <!-- ─── Homogeneous theorem ─── -->

  <theorem xml:id="thm-homogeneous-linear-ode">
    <title>General Solution of the Homogeneous Linear First-Order ODE</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>A(t)</m> be an antiderivative of <m>a(t)</m>,
        i.e. <m>A'(t) = a(t)</m>.
        The <em>general solution</em> of the homogeneous ODE
        <m>y'(t) + a(t)\, y(t) = 0</m> is
        <me>
          y(t) = C\, e^{-A(t)}, \qquad C \in \R.
        </me>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        The homogeneous ODE is separable: for <m>y \neq 0</m>,
        <m>y'/y = -a(t)</m>.
        Integrating: <m>\ln|y| = -A(t) + \text{const}</m>,
        so <m>|y| = e^{-A(t)+\text{const}}</m>.
        Absorbing the sign and the constant into <m>C</m> gives
        <m>y(t) = C e^{-A(t)}</m>,
        and the zero solution corresponds to <m>C = 0</m>.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </theorem>

  <!-- ─── Discharging capacitor ─── -->

  <example xml:id="ex-discharging-capacitor">
    <title>Discharging a Capacitor (RC Circuit)</title>
    <p>
      An RC series circuit consists of a resistor with resistance <m>R</m>
      and a capacitor with capacitance <m>C</m> connected in a loop.
      When the capacitor is pre-charged to voltage <m>U_0</m> at time
      <m>t = 0</m> and the circuit is closed with no external source,
      the capacitor voltage <m>U(t)</m> satisfies
      <me>
        U'(t) + \frac{1}{RC}\, U(t) = 0.
      </me>
      This is a homogeneous linear first-order ODE with the constant
      coefficient <m>a = \frac{1}{RC}</m>.
    </p>
    <figure xml:id="fig-rc-discharge">
      <caption>
        RC series circuit: a resistor <m>R</m> and capacitor <m>C</m>
        in a closed loop.  No external voltage source is present.
      </caption>
      <image xml:id="image-rc-discharge" width="40%">
        <latex-image>
          \begin{tikzpicture}[thick, font=\small]
            % Outer rectangle: circuit loop
            \draw (0,0) rectangle (4,2.5);
            % Capacitor symbol on the bottom edge (centre)
            \draw (1.5,0) -- (1.8,0);
            \draw[line width=2.5pt] (1.8,-0.18) -- (1.8,0.18);
            \draw[line width=2.5pt] (2.2,-0.18) -- (2.2,0.18);
            \draw (2.2,0) -- (2.5,0);
            \node[below] at (2,0) {$C$};
            % Resistor symbol on the top edge (centre)
            \draw (1.3,2.5) -- (1.5,2.5);
            \draw (1.5,2.3) rectangle (2.5,2.7);
            \draw (2.5,2.5) -- (2.7,2.5);
            \node[above] at (2,2.7) {$R$};
            % Switch on left edge
            \filldraw (0,1.1) circle (1.5pt);
            \filldraw (0,1.5) circle (1.5pt);
            \draw (0,1.1) -- (0.25,1.45);
            \node[left] at (0,1.3) {SW};
          \end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>
      With <m>A(t) = \frac{t}{RC}</m>, the theorem gives
      <me>
        U(t) = C_1\, e^{-t/(RC)}.
      </me>
      Applying the initial condition <m>U(0) = U_0</m> yields <m>C_1 = U_0</m>.
      The particular solution is
      <me>
        U(t) = U_0\, e^{-t/(RC)},
      </me>
      describing exponential decay with time constant <m>\tau = RC</m>.
    </p>
  </example>

  <!-- ─── Variation of constants ─── -->

  <p>
    Next, we turn from homogeneous equations to inhomogeneous linear ODEs of
    first order.
  </p>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-variation-of-constants">
    <title>Variation of Constants</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Fix a point <m>t_0</m> in the interval of interest and set
        <me>
          A(t) = \int_{t_0}^{t} a(s)\,ds.
        </me>
        The <em>general solution</em> of the inhomogeneous linear ODE
        <m>y'(t) + a(t)\, y(t) = b(t)</m> is
        <me>
          y(t) = e^{-A(t)}
          \left(C + \int_{t_0}^{t} e^{A(s)} b(s)\,ds\right),
          \qquad C \in \R.
        </me>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <remark xml:id="rem-solution-structure">
    <title>Structure of the General Solution</title>
    <p>
      The formula above decomposes naturally into two parts:
      <me>
        y(t) = \underbrace{C\, e^{-A(t)}}_{y_h(t)}
             + \underbrace{e^{-A(t)}
                 \int_{t_0}^{t} e^{A(s)} b(s)\,ds}_{y_p(t)}.
      </me>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>y_h(t) = C\, e^{-A(t)}</m> is the <em>general solution of the
            corresponding homogeneous ODE</em>; it contains the free constant
            <m>C</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>y_p(t) = e^{-A(t)}
            \int_{t_0}^{t} e^{A(s)} b(s)\,ds</m> is a
            <em>particular solution</em>
            of the inhomogeneous ODE.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      This additive structure — general homogeneous solution plus a particular
      inhomogeneous solution — recurs in Section 3-2 for higher-order linear
      ODEs.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <proof>
    <title>Derivation of Variation of Constants</title>
    <p>
      The general homogeneous solution is <m>y_h = C e^{-A(t)}</m>.
      We <q>vary the constant</q> by replacing <m>C</m> with an unknown
      function <m>u(t)</m>:
      <me>
        y(t) = u(t)\, e^{-A(t)}.
      </me>
      Differentiating and substituting into
      <m>y' + a(t) y = b(t)</m>:
      <md>
        <mrow>
          y'(t)
          \amp= u'(t)\, e^{-A(t)} + u(t)\cdot(-a(t))\, e^{-A(t)},
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          y' + a(t) y
          \amp= u'(t)\, e^{-A(t)}
               - a(t) u(t) e^{-A(t)}
               + a(t) u(t) e^{-A(t)}
           = u'(t)\, e^{-A(t)}.
        </mrow>
      </md>
      The two <m>a(t)</m>-terms cancel, leaving
      <m>u'(t)\, e^{-A(t)} = b(t)</m>, i.e.
      <me>
        u'(t) = b(t)\, e^{A(t)}.
      </me>
      Integrating from <m>t_0</m> to <m>t</m> gives
      <me>
        u(t) = C + \int_{t_0}^{t} b(s)\, e^{A(s)}\,ds.
      </me>
      Substituting back gives the stated formula.
    </p>
  </proof>

  <!-- ─── Charging capacitor ─── -->

  <example xml:id="ex-charging-capacitor">
    <title>Charging a Capacitor (RC Circuit with Voltage Source)</title>
    <p>
      Now assume the capacitor is uncharged at <m>t = 0</m> and a constant
      voltage source <m>U_0</m> is switched in.
      The governing ODE becomes inhomogeneous:
      <me>
        U'(t) + \frac{1}{RC}\, U(t) = \frac{U_0}{RC}.
      </me>
    </p>
    <figure xml:id="fig-rc-charge">
      <caption>
        RC circuit with a constant voltage source <m>U_0</m>:
        the capacitor charges from 0 towards <m>U_0</m>.
      </caption>
      <image xml:id="image-rc-charge" width="40%">
        <latex-image>
          \begin{tikzpicture}[thick, font=\small]
            \draw (0,0) rectangle (4,2.5);
            % Capacitor
            \draw (1.5,0) -- (1.8,0);
            \draw[line width=2.5pt] (1.8,-0.18) -- (1.8,0.18);
            \draw[line width=2.5pt] (2.2,-0.18) -- (2.2,0.18);
            \draw (2.2,0) -- (2.5,0);
            \node[below] at (2,0) {$C$};
            % Resistor
            \draw (1.3,2.5) -- (1.5,2.5);
            \draw (1.5,2.3) rectangle (2.5,2.7);
            \draw (2.5,2.5) -- (2.7,2.5);
            \node[above] at (2,2.7) {$R$};
            % Voltage source on left edge
            \draw (0,0.8) circle (0.3);
            \node at (0,0.8) {\scriptsize$+$};
            \node[left] at (-0.35,0.8) {$U_0$};
          \end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>
      We apply variation of constants with <m>t_0=0</m>,
      <m>a(t) = \frac{1}{RC}</m>,
      <m>A(t) = \int_0^t \frac{1}{RC}\,ds = \frac{t}{RC}</m>,
      <m>b(t) = \frac{U_0}{RC}</m>:
      <me>
        \int_0^t \frac{U_0}{RC}\, e^{s/RC}\,ds
        = U_0\bigl(e^{t/RC}-1\bigr).
      </me>
      Hence
      <me>
        U(t) = e^{-t/RC}\left(C_1 + U_0\bigl(e^{t/RC}-1\bigr)\right)
             = U_0 + (C_1-U_0)\, e^{-t/RC}.
      </me>
      Applying <m>U(0) = 0</m>: <m>C_1 = 0</m>.
      The particular solution is
      <me>
        U(t) = U_0\!\left(1 - e^{-t/(RC)}\right),
      </me>
      an exponential rise from <m>0</m> towards the source voltage <m>U_0</m>
      with the same time constant <m>\tau = RC</m>.
    </p>
    <figure xml:id="fig-capacitor-voltage">
      <caption>
        Capacitor voltage during charging:
        exponential approach from 0 to <m>U_0</m>.
        The time constant <m>\tau = RC</m> governs the speed.
      </caption>
      <image xml:id="image-capacitor-voltage" width="65%">
        <latex-image>
          \begin{tikzpicture}
            \begin{axis}[
              width=9cm, height=5.5cm,
              xlabel={$t/RC$}, ylabel={$U(t)/U_0$},
              xmin=0, xmax=5, ymin=0, ymax=1.25,
              axis lines=left,
              ytick={0, 1},
              yticklabels={$0$, $U_0$},
              samples=120, smooth
            ]
              \addplot[thick, blue, domain=0:5] {1 - exp(-x)};
              \addplot[dashed, gray, domain=0:5] {1};
              \addplot[dotted, gray] coordinates {(1,0)(1,0.632)};
              \addplot[dotted, gray] coordinates {(0,0.632)(1,0.632)};
              \node[right] at (axis cs:4.6,1.06) {$U_0$};
              \node[below right] at (axis cs:1.05,0) {$\tau$};
              \node[right] at (axis cs:2.2,0.55)
                {$U_0(1-e^{-t/RC})$};
            \end{axis}
          \end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>
      After one time constant <m>t = \tau = RC</m>, the voltage has reached
      <m>U(RC) = U_0(1 - e^{-1}) \approx 0.63\, U_0</m>,
      a useful rule of thumb in circuit design.
    </p>
  </example>

  <exercises xml:id="exercises-linear-first-order-odes-check">
    <title>Check Your Understanding</title>

    <exercise xml:id="exer-linear-first-order-identify">
      <title>Identify a Linear First-Order ODE</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Which equation is a linear first-order ODE?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p><m>y'+2t\,y=\sin t</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Correct. It has the form <m>y'+a(t)y=b(t)</m>.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>y'+y^2=0</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect. The term <m>y^2</m> makes the equation nonlinear.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>yy'+t=0</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect. The product <m>yy'</m> is nonlinear.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>y''+y=0</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect. This equation is second order.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="exer-linear-first-order-homogeneous-solution">
      <title>Solve a Homogeneous Linear ODE</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve
          <me>
            y'+y=0.
          </me>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          This is a homogeneous linear first-order ODE. Rewriting gives
          <me>
            y'=-y.
          </me>
          Separating variables,
          <me>
            \frac{1}{y}\,dy = -dt.
          </me>
          Integration yields
          <me>
            \ln|y|=-t+C.
          </me>
          Hence
          <me>
            y(t)=Ce^{-t},
            \qquad C\in\R.
          </me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>

</subsection>
